            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Arabic for a dinghy, then the Arabic for to row."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: نفق",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "نفق — nafaq — \"a tunnel\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "دخل القطار نفقا طويلا تحت الجبل. dakhala al-qiṭār nafaqan ṭawīlan taḥta al-jabal. The train went into a long tunnel under the mountain."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Plural: أنفاق, anfāq."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "نفق (nafaq) is built on the native Arabic root n-f-q, \"to pass away, to run out\" (Lane). Lane gives nafaq as a passage under the ground."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for getting around."
            }
          ]
        },
